      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. When Ray climbs \\( 4 \\) steps at a time, there are \\( 3 \\) steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n\n2. When Ray climbs \\( 5 \\) steps at a time, there are \\( 2 \\) steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nAdditionally, \\( n \\) must be greater than \\( 10 \\).\n\nWe start by finding a general solution to each congruence:\n1. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nFrom the first congruence, we have:\n\\[\nn = 4k + 3\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\\[\n4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we have:\n\\[\n4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 4, which is reversible in modular arithmetic because 4 and 5 are coprime:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nTherefore, \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substitute back to find \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = 4k + 3 = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7\n\\]\n\nThe general solution for \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\nn = 20m + 7\n\\]\nWe need the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( n > 10 \\). Substituting \\( m = 0 \\) (the smallest integer greater than 0):\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7\n\\]\nHowever, this is not greater than 10. So, we need to check \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27\n\\]\nSince 27 is indeed greater than 10, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies all conditions and is greater than 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]",
